    {
      "commentary": {
        "commtxt": "B0$ [On whether England need to address their difficulty in dealing with lower-order batters] Yeah, and I think it's becoming more and more of a theme across the board in modern cricket, guys can bat further down. And we'll do our work, and we'll make sure we have plans in place. And I'd say there wasn't much to work with in terms of the information on Razaullah there, but yeah, we'll make sure we do our work and we're better prepared for South Africa. [On batters not able to convert fifties into hundreds this series] I think everyone would be wanting to go on if you get yourself in. But I do also think that's been the nature of the summer, and the surfaces we've had. It's been across the board, it's not just been necessarily us. So the last couple of days we've seen a more normal Test cricket really. You've had to work harder for your wickets. Guys, if they got in, can score quite freely, and you feel like you can get a rhythm for batting again. So I think the pleasing thing is 2 for 2, for us to be able to see that home with two guys set and not out at the end, is a step forward in that direction too. So it would almost be more concerning if we were relying on two individuals that are scoring big hundreds consistently. The fact that everyone's contributing shows that they're good enough to perform at this level, I think is a really good thing too. And of course we'll have different challenges in the winter, different surfaces, but can we keep moving forward and take another stride forward as a group, and that being one of them, guys going on and making those big contributions? I mean, we still got 400 twice in these conditions. So a lot of good stuff there too.",
        "timestamp": 1789217561498,
        "overnum": 0.0,
        "inningsid": 4,
        "eventtype": "NONE",
